Observability → Monitoring system health and performance using telemetry data (logs, metrics, traces).
Incident Response → Detecting, triaging, and resolving issues when anomalies or failures occur.

AI helps automate both by correlating signals, predicting failures, and recommending remediations.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| AI Function                   | Use Case                                                      | Example                                                                           |
| ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Anomaly Detection**         | Identify outliers in metrics or logs before outages occur.    | Detect CPU spikes or latency anomalies using unsupervised ML.                     |
| **Root Cause Analysis (RCA)** | Correlate events/logs to pinpoint cause of incidents.         | Identify that “high DB latency” was triggered by “memory leak in microservice X.” |
| **Event Correlation**         | Group related alerts into one “incident” instead of hundreds. | Group 50 alerts from Kubernetes pods under one “Node Down” event.                 |
| **Predictive Insights**       | Forecast potential failures or capacity issues.               | Predict disk exhaustion 3 hours before it happens.                                |
| **Log Intelligence (NLP)**    | Summarize, cluster, and analyze unstructured logs.            | LLMs summarize recurring error logs and provide probable causes.                  |
| **Automated Remediation**     | Trigger corrective actions automatically.                     | Restart service, scale pods, or roll back versions via AI playbooks.              |

